Global Scope: Variables declared outside of any function or block have global scope. 
They are accessible from anywhere in the JavaScript program, including inside functions and blocks.

const globalVar = "I am global";
function logGlobal() {
  console.log(globalVar);
}
logGlobal();
console.log(globalVar);


```````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````

Function (Local) Scope: Variables declared within a function are only accessible within that specific function.
They are created when the function starts and deleted when it finishes.

function calculateTotal() {
  const price = 100; 
  console.log(price); 
}
calculateTotal();
// console.log(price); // ReferenceError: price is not defined


`````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````

Block Scope: Introduced in ES6, variables declared with let or const inside a set of curly braces {}
(e.g., in if statements, for loops, or standalone blocks) are only accessible within that block. 
Variables declared with var do not have block scope and "leak" into the surrounding function or global scope.
if (true) {
  let blockVar = "I am block-scoped";
  var functionVar = "I escape the block";
  console.log(blockVar); // Accessible
}
// console.log(blockVar); // ReferenceError: blockVar is not defined
console.log(functionVar); // Accessible (due to var's function/global scope)


```````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````````
Lexical Scope (Scope Chain): Inner functions can access variables defined in their outer (enclosing) functions' scopes.
JavaScript looks for a variable starting in the current scope and moving outward through parent scopes until it reaches the global scope. 
Key Declaration Keywords
Keyword 	Scope Type(s)	Hoisting Behavior	Redeclaration/Reassignment
var	Function/Global	Hoisted to the top of its scope	Can be redeclared and reassigned
let	Block/Function/Global	Not hoisted (Temporal Dead Zone)	Can be reassigned, but not redeclared
const	Block/Function/Global	Not hoisted (Temporal Dead Zone)	Cannot be reassigned or redeclared (constant)
